    dict(key='14-act-5', kind='console', title='Act Five — The limit of recursion',
         body='FIVE. The limit of recursion.\n  binarySearch on 1,000,000: 20 comparisons, depth 20: halving keeps the stack small\n  linearSearch on 1,000,000: StackOverflowError, one frame per element\n  the same loop in the static-array project needs O(1) extra space and finishes\n  Java does not remove tail calls, so even tail-recursive code keeps every frame',
         narration='And the limit of recursion. [[slnc 400]] Binary search on a million elements goes only twenty calls deep, because each call halves the range. [[slnc 500]] Linear search on the same million needs one frame per element. [[slnc 400]] The call stack runs out, and Java throws a stack overflow error. [[slnc 500]] The loop version finishes, with order one extra memory. [[slnc 400]] So, recurse when the depth is small, and use a loop for long linear passes.'),
